<p><span lang="EN-GB">Triangles are flat and have two faces, front and back. Sometimes it is impossible to determine the correct facing of a triangle: front or back and the result is distortion in the object view. In such cases the command</span> <span class="Command">Flip Normals</span> <span lang="EN-GB">is very useful. This command 'flips' or swaps the front and back faces. The difference between them is that</span> <span class="Command">Invert Normals</span> <span lang="EN-GB">inverts normals of the entire entity even if it is partially selected while</span> <span class="Command">Flip Normals</span> <span lang="EN-GB">inverts normals of the selection only. Another difference is that when you apply</span> <span class="Command">Flip Normals</span><span lang="EN-GB">, KUBE will register that the data are modified and when close them it will offer you to save the file. You can view the direction of normals using</span> <span class="Command">Display Normals</span> <span lang="EN-GB">from the</span> <span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>View</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">menu.</span></p>
<p class="Use"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Use</b></span> <span class="Command">Flip Normals</span> <span lang="EN-GB">for exporting and importing objects, which are backwards.</span></p>
